WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.901 --> 00:00:02.000
-I'm so happy to see you.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.501
I feel like it's almost --
-It's tradition.

00:00:04.501 --> 00:00:05.934
-Yes.
I was just gonna bring that up.

00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:07.267
Around this time
is when you visit us.

00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:08.734
-Same time next year.

00:00:08.734 --> 00:00:10.634
-I'm so excited. Thank you.
-Yeah.

00:00:10.634 --> 00:00:12.601
-We're November pals. Bonded.
-So happy to be here.

00:00:12.601 --> 00:00:14.000
-You have --
I want to talk about this

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:15.834
'cause it's very exciting.
You have a new family member.

00:00:15.834 --> 00:00:17.901
-I do.
-This is very exciting.

00:00:17.901 --> 00:00:20.667
-I do.
-It's a puppy named Dot?

00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:21.934
-She's so cute.
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:00:21.934 --> 00:00:24.868
-She was a pound and a half here
when I brought her home.

00:00:24.868 --> 00:00:26.968
-A pound and --
-And, you know, it sounds like,

00:00:26.968 --> 00:00:29.133
"Oh, so cute.
I can take her everywhere,

00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:31.701
and I can put her
in my purse," you know?

00:00:31.701 --> 00:00:33.868
But the truth is, then you get
her home and you realize,

00:00:33.868 --> 00:00:37.100
"Oh, my God, she's so little."
-Too little.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.434
-And it's so --
kind of too little,

00:00:38.434 --> 00:00:40.767
because I was afraid
I was gonna step on her.

00:00:40.767 --> 00:00:42.367
You know, she'd die.
-Yeah.

00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:45.601
-And -- And she would
get in places and...

00:00:45.601 --> 00:00:47.601
-Yeah, we had a dog once
that got, like,

00:00:47.601 --> 00:00:50.100
lost behind, like, a radiator.
-Oh!

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.133
-And it wasn't --
it wasn't on or anything.

00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:54.834
But you get afraid.
It's too little.

00:00:54.834 --> 00:00:57.601
Also, puppies are hard.
-Puppies -- Oh, my God.

00:00:57.601 --> 00:00:59.868
She's so little.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:59.868 --> 00:01:01.234
"Puppies are..."
-They're so hard,

00:01:01.234 --> 00:01:02.834
they're so hard.
-They are really hard.

00:01:02.834 --> 00:01:05.000
-It's like childbirth, you know?
You forget about the pain.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.100
You would never do it again.
-No, yeah.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.634
And then you do it again.
-And then you do it again.

00:01:09.634 --> 00:01:12.067
But, yeah, because she --

00:01:12.067 --> 00:01:14.767
And it's -- having --
it's worse than having --

00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:16.167
People say,
"Oh, it's like having a baby."

00:01:16.167 --> 00:01:18.601
It's worse.
-Yeah, babies can wear diapers.

00:01:18.601 --> 00:01:21.334
-And they lay there.
And they lay there.

00:01:21.334 --> 00:01:22.667
-Yeah.
-And you feed them,

00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:24.033
you change their d--
and you hold them

00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:25.367
for, like, a long time.

00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:26.367
-Yeah.
-Right?

00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:27.234
-Dogs...
-And even though

00:01:27.234 --> 00:01:28.634
you don't sleep, you're not --

00:01:28.634 --> 00:01:31.901
you're not chasing them around,
cleaning up their poop,

00:01:31.901 --> 00:01:33.701
and they're not chewing
on your clothes.

00:01:33.701 --> 00:01:36.467
-You think your dog
looks like someone, though.

00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:39.000
-Yeah, yeah.
In the mornings.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.334
-In the mornings,
when Dot wakes up.

00:01:40.334 --> 00:01:42.300
-When she wakes up...
-You think she looks like....

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.534
-...she looks like Rod Stewart.
-Yeah.

00:01:44.534 --> 00:01:47.334
And we brought a photo,
a side-by-side photo.

00:01:47.334 --> 00:01:50.033
Let's take a look at this.
I think you're right.

00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:51.934
Not bad.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:51.934 --> 00:01:55.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:55.701 --> 00:01:57.300
Sexy!

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.567
You also have a big milestone
coming up.

00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:04.400
You're going to be married
to your husband for 30 years.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.968
-Yeah, 30 years.
-Holy moly.

00:02:05.968 --> 00:02:07.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:07.667 --> 00:02:09.968
-Yeah.
I know.

00:02:09.968 --> 00:02:11.701
-How do you --
How do you celebrate 30?

00:02:11.701 --> 00:02:13.434
-30. The sa--
Eh, the same way.

00:02:13.434 --> 00:02:14.667
We go to dinner.
-Yeah.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:17.067
-I mean -- I mean,
I thought about having --

00:02:17.067 --> 00:02:20.167
I can maybe weave in the 30
years we've been together,

00:02:20.167 --> 00:02:23.567
I've had maybe five parties.
-Really?

00:02:23.567 --> 00:02:26.467
Yeah, but I just --
I think we like

00:02:26.467 --> 00:02:28.968
just being together and quiet

00:02:28.968 --> 00:02:30.634
and having a little wine and...

00:02:30.634 --> 00:02:32.868
-Do you have date nights?
-We do.

00:02:32.868 --> 00:02:34.834
We've always had
Saturday night date nights

00:02:34.834 --> 00:02:37.567
from the time that we met,

00:02:37.567 --> 00:02:39.434
and I look forward
to it all week.

00:02:39.434 --> 00:02:41.634
That's my highlight of my week.
-Oh, I love that.

00:02:41.634 --> 00:02:43.367
-Mm-hmm.
Is that, like, one of

00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:45.234
the secrets to your marriage?

00:02:45.234 --> 00:02:47.267
-I think so.
I think it's -- well, I think

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:48.734
it's definitely
carving out time.

00:02:48.734 --> 00:02:53.067
Especially because, when we met,

00:02:53.067 --> 00:02:58.400
my daughter had been
with me for...

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.767
Well, David and I had only been
together for two months,

00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:06.567
and she arrived, and we were
kind of parents right away.

00:03:06.567 --> 00:03:09.901
So I think that the honeymoon
period for us was --

00:03:09.901 --> 00:03:11.634
was kind of interrupted.

00:03:11.634 --> 00:03:13.133
-Yeah.
-And so we're stuck.

00:03:13.133 --> 00:03:14.834
-You just had to raise a child.

00:03:14.834 --> 00:03:18.534
But I think we also --
I'm just very amused by him.

00:03:18.534 --> 00:03:22.067
And he still surprises me.

00:03:22.067 --> 00:03:25.100
And I think we're just both
kind of intrigued by each other.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:26.300
-Yeah, you're still interested
in each other.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:27.868
-Yeah. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:27.868 --> 00:03:32.334
I want to ask you about this
very successful business

00:03:32.334 --> 00:03:34.367
you're doing right here,
Henry Rose.

00:03:34.367 --> 00:03:36.634
This one -- this package
is called the Slumberparty.

00:03:36.634 --> 00:03:40.300
[ Cheers and applause ]
Now, this is a fragrance.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.300
-This is a fragrance company.
-A fragrance company.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:43.901
Look at how gorgeous this is.
-Yeah.

00:03:43.901 --> 00:03:47.968
It's the -- the first
fully transparent,

00:03:47.968 --> 00:03:53.033
EWG-verified line of premium,
genderless fine fragrances.

00:03:53.033 --> 00:03:54.734
-Congratulations on this.
-Thank you.

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:55.734
-I want to --

00:03:55.734 --> 00:03:59.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:59.734 --> 00:04:02.634
And they're not -- they're
not named like, you know,

00:04:02.634 --> 00:04:05.334
like, after a scent
or something.

00:04:05.334 --> 00:04:08.367
It's more of an experience
that you've had.

00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:10.167
-Well, I learned
through the process,

00:04:10.167 --> 00:04:12.367
because when I started,
I started working --

00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:14.868
because nobody else
would actually work with me.

00:04:14.868 --> 00:04:16.300
I started work--
'Cause everyone thought

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.300
I was nuts,
which I'm a little bit.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.300
But I started working
directly with the perfumers,

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:23.834
and I had to learn
a whole new language

00:04:23.834 --> 00:04:27.767
and how to talk about it.
And they say, "Don't, don't,

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:29.567
don't tell us
what you think it smells like

00:04:29.567 --> 00:04:32.033
because it will
only confuse us."

00:04:32.033 --> 00:04:34.400
So they said, "Tell us,
where does it take you,

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.934
where does it transport you?"

00:04:37.934 --> 00:04:41.901
And I -- And so the very first
fragrance that we locked

00:04:41.901 --> 00:04:46.300
is Torn.
It's vanilla and, like --

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.234
It's like a spicy vanilla.
-Mm.

00:04:48.234 --> 00:04:49.801
-And what I didn't realize,

00:04:49.801 --> 00:04:51.734
but I learned through
the process,

00:04:51.734 --> 00:04:53.734
was I was actually
going after the scent

00:04:53.734 --> 00:04:56.067
of my father's cologne.

00:04:56.067 --> 00:04:58.267
And I always really loved --
You know,

00:04:58.267 --> 00:05:01.033
because scent memory
is really, really powerful.

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:02.367
You know, people talk about it,

00:05:02.367 --> 00:05:04.400
but I didn't really --
it truly is.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:05.701
It's scientific.
-Oh, yeah.

00:05:05.701 --> 00:05:06.968
I mean,
go back to your high school

00:05:06.968 --> 00:05:08.801
and you just smell
the high school halls,

00:05:08.801 --> 00:05:10.868
and you're like, "Oh, my gosh.
I got to get out of here."

00:05:10.868 --> 00:05:13.467
Yeah, it's...
-It's actually in --

00:05:13.467 --> 00:05:15.467
The late Peggy Feury,
is something we used to use

00:05:15.467 --> 00:05:18.167
in acting class,
which was an item of something,

00:05:18.167 --> 00:05:19.534
like, maybe your father's,
you know?

00:05:19.534 --> 00:05:21.200
Like your father's old sweater
or something

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:23.167
that can stir you up.

00:05:23.167 --> 00:05:24.734
-Flora Carnivora.

00:05:24.734 --> 00:05:29.133
-Flora Carnivora.
Um...

00:05:29.133 --> 00:05:33.400
brings me back to when
I was a little thief.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:35.467
[ Laughter ]

00:05:35.467 --> 00:05:37.267
-What was --
Was your character --

00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:39.400
Is your thief name
Flora Carnivora?

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:42.934
-No, no, I was little
and roaming the neighborhood,

00:05:42.934 --> 00:05:45.133
which I shouldn't
have been, probably.

00:05:45.133 --> 00:05:46.834
And I would sneak into
my neighbor's garden

00:05:46.834 --> 00:05:48.734
because we didn't
have any flowers,

00:05:48.734 --> 00:05:50.567
and they were too much trouble
for my dad.

00:05:50.567 --> 00:05:53.100
-Yeah.
-And I would sneak into

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:57.167
their garden, and --
and I -- and one day,

00:05:57.167 --> 00:05:59.801
I got up the courage
to steal some.

00:05:59.801 --> 00:06:02.868
And I ran home,
and I put them in a bowl,

00:06:02.868 --> 00:06:06.234
and I crushed them up --
[ Inhales deeply ]

00:06:06.234 --> 00:06:08.000
And I was just intox--
the smell.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.868
And then, of course,
it started to rot.

00:06:09.868 --> 00:06:11.234
-Yeah. Of course.
Yeah.

00:06:11.234 --> 00:06:13.734
-And was really stinky.
So I was very disappointed.

00:06:13.734 --> 00:06:15.834
But that was my first
failed foray

00:06:15.834 --> 00:06:17.334
into perfumery.
-Perfume. Yeah.

00:06:17.334 --> 00:06:18.601
-What is this one
that's upside down?

00:06:18.601 --> 00:06:20.200
Why is it upside down?

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:22.934
-Because it doesn't behave.

00:06:22.934 --> 00:06:24.501
[ Laughter ]
-This one?

00:06:24.501 --> 00:06:25.634
-It doesn't behave.

00:06:25.634 --> 00:06:28.400
It's, um -- it's --

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.667
It's a menace.
-Mm.

00:06:30.667 --> 00:06:33.267
It's called Menace, yeah.
-It's called Menace.

00:06:33.267 --> 00:06:34.534
And --
-What does it smell like?

00:06:34.534 --> 00:06:37.267
-I actually think -- you know,
if I were gonna

00:06:37.267 --> 00:06:39.100
pick a cologne for you...

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:40.334
-[ Laughing ] What?
[ Laughter ]

00:06:40.334 --> 00:06:42.834
Why?! I'm not a menace.
I'm very well-behaved.

00:06:42.834 --> 00:06:43.901
-I just think -- I don't know.

00:06:43.901 --> 00:06:46.367
I imagine, like,
a Dennis the Menace thing

00:06:46.367 --> 00:06:48.234
that maybe went on with you
when you were little.

00:06:48.234 --> 00:06:51.334
-Never getting in trouble.
-And also the olfactive here,

00:06:51.334 --> 00:06:52.601
I think, that you would
really love.

00:06:52.601 --> 00:06:53.634
-Really?
-Now, what have we got here?

00:06:53.634 --> 00:06:59.968
-But it is an aquatic musk.

00:06:59.968 --> 00:07:05.467
So I think you need to apply it
doing the dolphin.

00:07:05.467 --> 00:07:08.367
-Now, we discussed,
the only way I put cologne on

00:07:08.367 --> 00:07:11.300
or any type of scent
is to dolphin into it.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:12.934
-Right, right.
-I spray it,

00:07:12.934 --> 00:07:14.601
and then I kind of get in.
-We know this about you.

00:07:14.601 --> 00:07:16.200
-All right. So now --
So this is Menace.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:17.834
-Yeah, this is Menace.
-All right. So do I have

00:07:17.834 --> 00:07:19.968
to do a --
-You have to do the dolphin.

00:07:19.968 --> 00:07:21.901
-A menacing dolphin?

00:07:21.901 --> 00:07:23.033
[ Laughter ]

00:07:23.033 --> 00:07:24.133
[ Imitates menacing dolphin
squeaking ]

00:07:24.133 --> 00:07:26.901
[ Laughter ]

00:07:26.901 --> 00:07:29.367
[ Imitates dolphin ]
Oh, wait. You spray.

00:07:29.367 --> 00:07:30.868
Here, you spray.
Yep. Go.

00:07:30.868 --> 00:07:31.701
-Ready?
-Yep.

00:07:31.701 --> 00:07:32.901
Okay.
-Go. Go.

00:07:32.901 --> 00:07:34.267
Go, go, go, go, go, go, go!

00:07:34.267 --> 00:07:35.834
[ Imitating dolphin squeaking ]

00:07:35.834 --> 00:07:40.334
[ Cheers and applause ]
Ooh.

00:07:40.334 --> 00:07:42.534
Wow!

00:07:42.534 --> 00:07:45.234
That is me!
That's my favorite right there.

00:07:45.234 --> 00:07:47.868
Ooh, I love it!
Michelle Pfeiffer, everybody!

00:07:47.868 --> 00:07:49.467
Whoo!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:49.467 --> 00:07:52.567
Check out Henry Rose
in stores nationwide

00:07:52.567 --> 00:07:54.434
and at henryrose.com.

00:07:54.434 --> 00:07:56.968
We'll be right back
with Bobby Flay. Stick around.

